# =============================================================================
# WARP-2574 — host artefact manifest
# =============================================================================
#
# Every artefact `install_single_box_host_integration` (scripts/lib/single-box.sh)
# places on a box, in one machine-readable table.
#
# ── WHY THIS FILE EXISTS ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# WARP-1829 shipped `droplet-host-units check`, which finds host units running
# STALE code. It enumerates units from systemd, so it can only ever see units
# that EXIST. A host artefact that was never installed has no unit to enumerate
# and no process to compare — it is invisible to that detector, and to
# `systemctl status`, and to /api/health.
#
# Measured 2026-08-31 on the bench box: WARP-2190 (droplet-power-restore) and
# WARP-2192 (the hardware watchdog) merged on 2026-08-26, the box's checkout
# contained both, and NEITHER was installed. Both units read `not-found`,
# /dev/watchdog0 did not exist, and the board's AC-loss policy was still
# `always-off` — the box would have stayed dark after a power cut, five days
# after the fix shipped. The box refresh updates the checkout and restarts
# CONTAINERS; nothing re-runs the host-integration installer, so a host-unit
# feature can merge, be marked Done, and run on zero boxes.
#
# This table is the expectation half of that reconciliation. `droplet-host-units
# audit` reads it and compares it against the live filesystem and
# `systemctl is-enabled`; the `host_artefacts` check on droplet-watchdog.timer
# reports the result.
#
# ── WHY IT CANNOT ROT ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# A hand-maintained table that drifts from the installer would be WORSE than no
# table: it would report green for artefacts nobody installs. So the table is
# not trusted on its own — tests/host-artefacts.test.sh reconciles it against
# `install_single_box_host_integration` in BOTH directions on every PR:
#
#   * every `install -m` / `install -d` / `systemctl enable` in that function
#     must have a row here, and
#   * every row here must name a source that exists in the repo.
#
# Adding a host artefact without a row FAILS CI. That is the structural closure:
# the blind spot cannot silently re-open.
#
# ── WHY IT IS READ FROM THE CHECKOUT, NEVER FROM AN INSTALLED COPY ───────────
# `audit` resolves this file inside the box's git checkout. It is deliberately
# NOT installed to /usr/local. The question being asked is "is the box running
# what the tree says", so the expectation must come from the TREE. An installed
# copy would be stale in exactly the situation the audit exists to catch — a
# checkout that pulled a new artefact whose installer never re-ran — and would
# answer "nothing is missing" while the new artefact sat uninstalled.
#
# ── FORMAT ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#   <kind>  <source>  <destination>  <mode>  <policy>  <note...>
#
# Whitespace-separated; `-` means "not applicable to this kind". The note is
# free text to end of line. Blank lines and `#` comments are ignored.
#
#   kind    file   a file the installer copies into place
#           dir    a directory the installer creates
#           unit   a systemd unit the installer ENABLES
#           skip   an artefact deliberately NOT reconciled (reason mandatory)
#
#   source  repo-relative path of the file the installer copies FROM
#           (`-` for dir/unit/skip rows)
#
#   policy  file: track     content AND mode must match the repo source
#           file: presence  must exist; content deliberately not compared
#                           (note mandatory — say WHY, or use `track`)
#           dir:  present   must exist as a directory
#           unit: enabled   `systemctl is-enabled` must say enabled
#           skip: <token>   short reason token; note mandatory
#
# A `presence` or `skip` row with no note fails the CI guard. You cannot opt out
# of content checking without writing down why.
#
# Adding a host artefact? Add its row here in the same commit as the installer
# line. The guard will tell you if you forget, but the point is that the box
# reports the gap on its own from then on.
# =============================================================================

